  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nAn area of high pressure over the region is producing cool \ntemperatures across the forecast area with readings in the 40s \nand 50s. These readings are 5 to 10 degrees below average for \nthis time of year. With the cool atmosphere, temperatures could \nfall quickly upon sunset, with reading in the 30s. Solely based \nthe forecast low temperatures, have issued a frost advisory for \nthe James River Valley, and a freeze warning further east. While\nmarginal conditions, will lean on the side of caution for these\nheadlines. As the surface high shifts southeast of the region, \nthe forecast area will see increasing southerly winds, with \ngusts possible exceeding 35 to 45 mph along the higher terrain \nareas. There is a 40-70% chance for wind gusts to be greater \nthan 45 mph along the eastern edge of the Sisseton Hills. The \nwinds, along with some temperature advection may cause \ntemperatures to become steady or slowly rise Saturday morning.\n\nWarmer temperatures will move into the area for the weekend with \nhighs reaching the upper 50s and 60s. The upper level flow pattern \nthrough next week favors above average temperatures and mostly dry \nconditions. A deepening trough along the west coast will cause upper \nlevel ridging over the Northern Plains through most of the next work \nweek. The west coast trough should move across the desert southwest \nand eventually into the area by the end of the work week and through \nnext weekend.",
